WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.790
There have been a number of high-profile plagiarism
scandals in politics.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:18.830
For example, in 2016, Melania Trump gave a
speech at the Republican National Convention,

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:23.400
that directly plagiarized a 2008 speech of
Michelle Obamas.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.850
Here some of the text from Melania Trump.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:02:18.319
You can see that it is a direct copy of Michelle
Obama's words.

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:22.340
Plagiarism is the practice of taking someone
else’s work or ideas and passing them off

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.160
as one's own.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.170
Most people are aware that you shouldn't do
this, that it's unethical to plagiarize.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:33.420
Where it gets murky, however, is that many
students aren't completely sure exactly what

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.730
constitutes plagiarism.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:42.010
In this presentation, we are going to try
to make that clear.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.510
What constitutes plagiarism?

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.090
Most people know that you shouldn't be stealing
large chunks of text from others, but plagiarism

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.810
can be more complex than that.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:56.010
Cutting and pasting a sentence, or even a
part of a sentence from another source, and

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:00.570
putting that into your own work as if you've
written it, that's plagiarism.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:04.920
Taking somebody else's work, and cutting and
pasting it into your document and then slightly

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:09.790
re-arranging the words or changing a word
here or there, that's plagiarism.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:17.480
Borrowing descriptions or definitions directly
from Wikipedia, that's plagiarism.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.450
Here is an example.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:22.390
This is a Wikipedia entry on Ernest Hemingway.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:27.819
It says, “Ernest Miller Hemingway was an
American author and journalist.

00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:32.790
His economical and understated style had a
strong influence on 20th century fiction,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.930
while his life of adventure and his public
image influenced later generations.”

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:41.500
Now, imagine a student is writing about Ernest
Hemingway.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:46.770
So, they go to Wikipedia, and they start cutting
and pasting bits and pieces from the Wikipedia

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:51.400
entry and they come up with the following:
“Ernest Hemingway's thrifty and understated

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:55.190
style strongly influenced 20th century fiction.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:04:00.379
His audacious lifestyle and public image also
influenced later generations.”

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:02.570
This is plagiarism.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:07.750
Notice that the student substituted a few
words: thrifty for economical, audacious lifestyle

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:12.280
for life of adventure, and rearranged a few
things and changed a few other things, like

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.580
“had a strong influence” to “strongly
influenced”.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.280
These changes are just cosmetic.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.030
These are not the student’s own words.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.500
These are not his or her thought.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.960
These are the words and thoughts of the original
author.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.960
One could have rewritten that text without
having any understanding of Hemingway or of

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:38.400
what a thrifty or understated style even means,
or what his lifestyle was.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.470
When you're writing about others' ideas or
writing from sources, you need to understand

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:47.460
and digest the material well enough so that
you can put it in your own words.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:51.740
This means you're probably going to need to
go to multiple sources to adequately understand

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.810
the subject matter.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:57.900
As you're collecting source material, it's
okay to cut and paste passages from other

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:02.720
people's work to get everything in one place,
like in one word document.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.680
But put those passages in quotation marks
so you don't later forget that those aren't

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.370
your words.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:12.720
And when you go to write about it, work from
memory, never start with someone else's text

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.120
and just move their words around.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.960
By the time you're ready to write, you should
understand the material well enough to be

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.270
able to start from scratch.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:25.960
Draw your own conclusions, come up with your
own ideas on the topic.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:31.490
That's what you need to do to avoid plagiarism.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:36.639
Another thing that students are often less
aware of is the concept of self-plagiarism.

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:41.020
Self-plagiarism is recycling your own writing
or your own data from one published paper

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.720
to the next.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.880
It's also unethical.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.110
In addition, you may be violating copyright
laws from the journal that owns the published

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.600
paper.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.550
You are not supposed to plagiarize even from
your own work.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:58.490
One possible exception to this is that there
may be some duplication of text within the

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:01.260
materials and methods section.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:06.009
For example, there may be only so many ways
to explain what you did to the cells.

00:06:06.009 --> 00:06:12.170
So, if you've used the same experiment across
multiple studies, there may be some text repeated.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:16.190
It shouldn't be a complete copy, but if you
explain some of the materials and methods

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:21.730
in the same way, in multiple papers, journal
editors are usually fine with that.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:26.440
But everything else should be new.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:30.870
Authors also sometimes engage in something
that's akin to plagiarism which is duplication

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:34.410
of their old results and their old data.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:38.130
Sometimes an author will take a data set that's
already published, and maybe add a little

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:43.940
bit of new data, or tweak the data slightly,
and present the whole thing as a new paper.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:47.710
This is misleading because it's putting things
out there as if they were two independent

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:51.860
pieces of information supporting a hypothesis
when there isn't.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:57.070
It's padding the medical literature with superfluous
papers, and there's also, of course, a copyright

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:59.320
issue.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:05.070
Interestingly, there are research being conducted
to go through the literature, and figure out

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:09.710
exactly how prevalent is plagiarism in the
scientific literature.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:14.280
There was a story in Nature, where they reported
that in pilot tests of the plagiarism detection

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:20.140
software CrossCheck, some journals found that
6% to 23% of submitted manuscripts had to

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.970
be rejected due to plagiarism.

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:27.860
That is a very high percentage.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:32.930
Another study used automatic detection software,
and then they manually confirmed plagiarism

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.070
in submitted papers.

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:40.419
They found that 8% of the papers had plagiarized
other people's work and about 3% involved

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:42.520
self-plagiarism.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:50.520
So again, we're ranging around 10% of the
papers having significant plagiarism.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:54.270
Another group of researchers did a study of
plagiarism in the personal statements for

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:56.759
residency applications.

00:07:56.759 --> 00:08:00.350
These were doctors who were applying to do
their residency.

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:06.160
The researchers used a plagiarism detection
software to review about 5000 personal statements,

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:09.699
and then they confirmed suspected plagiarism
manually.

00:08:09.699 --> 00:08:14.300
They found that 5% of essays had clear evidence
of plagiarism.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:18.040
Meaning 1 in 20 of the doctors applying for
residency had plagiarized.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:22.320
So, plagiarism is a significant problem.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:30.509
Be careful in your own work to put everything
in your own words.

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:35.690
In summary, in order to write something original,
you have to go to enough sources so that you

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:39.610
can figure out for yourself, about the topic
you are writing.

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:43.839
You have to understand enough to be able to
generate new ideas, and that's what you need

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:45.480
to do to avoid plagiarism.

